WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.559 --> 00:00:05.100
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Hello everyone.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.300
Good afternoon.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.730
And thank you for coming
to this session IoT

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.840
and Cloud for
industrial application.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.670
I am Indranil Chakraborty.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:20.040
I'm a product guy at Google
Cloud Platform, working on IoT.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.020
What we're going to
cover today, we're

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.250
going to cover a couple of
industrial applications, which

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.520
are enabled by IoT
and Google Cloud,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.090
and give you some
demos by our partners

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.180
and our customers on how
they have leveraged the power

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.220
of Google Cloud with IoT.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.370
To really solve some
interesting industrial problems.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.950
Let's get started.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.020
I'm sure you all have heard
about the fourth Industrial

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.620
Revolution.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.860
If you look it up
on Wikipedia, it

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.250
said that we've been in this
fourth Industrial Revolution

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.560
for quite a few years.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.080
The first revolution started
back in the 18th century,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.860
when steam engine
was first invented.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:16.920
And, powered by water
and steam power,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.490
it was used to
mobilize and automate

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:25.500
a lot of the production as part
of Industrial Revolution 1.0.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.190
The second revolution
started with mass production

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:34.230
of assembly line, using
automation in the manufacturing

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.430
field.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.230
It was primarily
started by Henry Ford

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.030
when he started assembly line
for his Ford car manufacturing.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.870
And he said that the third one
started with computerization

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.900
and automation of IT.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.840
So as we started installing
robots and connecting

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.150
those robots to the
IT system, that's

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.540
what started the third
Industrial Revolution.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.000
And the fourth revolution
has been going on

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.210
for over 50 years, thanks
to the massive advances

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.310
in technologies.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:10.289
And we believe that
with IoT and Cloud,

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.030
we can make further advancement
in the fourth Industrial

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.630
Revolution stage, which
can help businesses derive

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:22.590
a lot more efficiency, and
get a large amount of value

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:23.940
from IoT and Cloud.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.330
What we're seeing
today in the industry

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.090
are couple of key
trends, which is

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:36.090
driving the advancement of
industrial application using

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.830
IoT.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.760
First, what we're seeing is
we're seeing an increasing

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.080
desire by the industry
players to combine data from

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.360
their operational sites--
so factories, utility grids,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.680
and other sites--

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.460
and combine it with
their IT systems,

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.000
so that they can really get the
value from both of these data

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.700
and derive a lot
more efficiency,

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.230
and improve their overall
production as well.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.680
So that has been one
of the key drivers

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.300
that we're finding
in the industry.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.740
The second is it it's
really become much easier

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.720
to extract data from factory and
other industrial sites as well.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.280
If you think about it, when you
say IoT in industrial scenario,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.320
a lot of the
manufacturers have already

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.540
had installed sensors
in their machines.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.280
So these machineries
were already

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.820
instrumented with sensors.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.190
And they're collecting
a large amount of data.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.530
But only a very small
percentage of this data

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.100
is actually getting utilized
for many of these applications.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.560
And so as the prices of
sensors keeps dropping,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.100
thanks to the mobile
phone revolution,

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.250
we're seeing a large number
of these industries are either

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.280
using these existing machines,
which are instrumented with

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.940
sensors, or retrofitting
machines-- legacy machine--

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.550
with sensors, which can
then be connected to Cloud

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.220
for deriving a lot of value.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.100
And finally, over the
last couple of years,

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.300
Cloud technology providers,
such as Google Cloud Platform,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:18.149
has done a lot of improvement
to ingest massive scale of data,

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:21.570
and then store it in a
very cost effective way.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:25.890
In addition, the Google
Cloud platform and others

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.500
have also been providing
machine learning capabilities,

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.470
so you can really extract
very meaningful insights

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.130
from this data.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.940
And so as we think about
industrial application,

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:41.504
there are a couple of use-cases
we need to think about.

00:04:41.504 --> 00:04:42.920
What are the key
use-cases we want

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.240
to address as we start
building IoT applications

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.000
for the industrial use-cases?

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.090
And so you may want
to have improved

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.510
your overall efficiency
of your product line.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:02.140
There are players
and customers who

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.790
really suffered a lot
if there is any downtime

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.140
in their missionaries.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:10.160
And so they want to use
data and apply analysis

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.310
on those data to
predict failure,

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.860
so they can avoid
any such downtime.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.410
Real time tracking of inventory
is also a key use-case,

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:24.820
because you can use that to not
just avoid inventory stock-out,

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.040
but you can also use
it to predict demand.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.470
So you can make
sure that there's

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:33.250
enough inventory as the
customer demand increases.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.380
And of course,
product quality is

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.530
one of the key areas where a lot
of our customers and partners

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.410
are very interested in as well--

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:44.730
asset tracking the supply
chain, among other ones as well.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:48.960
In Google, as we think
about these use-cases,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.740
and as a developer or as
a industrial partner--

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.130
as you think about building
applications, IT applications

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:58.685
for industries, there
are really two key areas

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.910
which you need to solve
in order to address

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:03.990
any of these use-cases.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.150
One is we have to be able to
ingest massive amounts of data

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:13.310
across different sites, and then
be able to process it at scale,

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.560
and then tease out key
insights so you can really

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.260
apply those learnings and
get some business value.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.950
And in Google Cloud,
we have a number

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.866
of these services, which
allows us to do that.

00:06:24.866 --> 00:06:26.240
And if you think
about it, Google

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.670
has been working on big data
for over a decade, right?

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.050
So we have been working on
ingesting data, processing data

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.600
at scale over a decade now.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.582
And we think we can
apply the same services

00:06:37.582 --> 00:06:39.290
for industrial
applications as well, when

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:41.600
it comes to the context of IoT.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.470
So I'm going to spend some time
on a couple of these product

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:49.280
offerings, which we have for
ingesting and processing--

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.690
analyzing data at scale.

00:06:53.214 --> 00:06:54.630
The first thing
you want to do is,

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:58.650
if you think about IoT and
data you get from sensors--

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.670
while individual
sensors which are either

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.690
instrumented in the machines,
may be generating couple

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:06.630
of bytes of data per
second or per few seconds.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.690
But when you look at it from
a cumulative perspective,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.440
it generates a massive
volume of data.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.600
And so you want a system which
can ingest data at scale--

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.580
massive amount of data at scale.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:19.800
And Pub/Sub is a great
product for this.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.300
Essentially, what
we do here is you

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.050
have IoT devices
or sensors which

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.320
can publish data, either
directly or via gateway,

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.560
to a particular topic.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.630
And then you can
have other services

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:38.930
to consume data from that
topic for further processing.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:42.460
A couple of key
benefits of Pub/Sub.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.000
One is it's durable
message persistence.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.910
And what does it mean?

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.720
The sensor data, since
it's constrained,

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:53.110
you need to be able to send the
data without much challenge,

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.620
and it shouldn't have to
try to resend it every time.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:00.160
So Pub/Sub makes sure that
once the data is published,

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.410
it is durably stored,
until the consumption party

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.180
sends an ACK back.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:11.710
The second advantage of Pub/Sub
is it's a global service.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.890
While your sensors
and industrial sites

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.770
might be distributed globally,
you want a central location.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:22.960
Your consumption must be
having in a central location.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.760
Since Pub/Sub is
a global service,

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:30.450
you don't have to worry about
what happens if my New York

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.420
service goes down?

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:34.659
Or what happens if my San
Jose service goes down?

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:37.539
It's a global service, and
we scale automatically.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:40.039
In fact, Pub/Sub can
scale from handling

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:43.720
a few thousands of data per
second to millions of messages

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.820
per second within just
a couple of minutes,

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.690
without user or the developer
needing to provision any

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:51.470
additional services.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:55.660
And finally, it also allows
you to separate your app

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.720
to your upstream
device deployment

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.610
from your downstream
application,

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:03.550
so you can continue
to build and iterate

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:06.310
on your application, which is
consuming the data from all

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:10.690
these different IoT
devices, without needing

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:13.660
to change or modify your
upstream deployment.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:15.850
In fact, you can use
it to route the data

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.120
to some of the Cloud services,
such as Data Flow, Cloud

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:19.840
Function.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:26.140
Or even your own existing
ETO's pipeline as well.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:30.270
Once you've ingested
the data at scale,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.860
the next thing you want to
do is really process it.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.330
And for industry
IoT applications,

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.210
real time analysis
and real time insights

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.570
become really critical.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.810
And so Data Flow
is a great product,

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.640
which allows you to constantly
receive data from Pub/Sub.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.410
Streams of data from
Pub/Sub, and then

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:54.850
continuously analyze it.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:59.260
So you could compute average
over a stream of data

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:00.550
within a certain window.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.970
You can even use it to
convert data format,

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:07.480
so you have devices across
different industrial sites.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.446
And most of the time, devices
have different data formats.

00:10:10.446 --> 00:10:12.070
And before you want
to store it, if you

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:13.840
want to convert it
into standard format,

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.942
Data Flow is a great
product for that as well.

00:10:16.942 --> 00:10:18.400
And in addition,
Data Flow can also

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.170
be used to get data
from other services,

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:27.880
such as your weather data
or traffic data as well.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.150
And BigQuery allows you to
store massive volumes of data

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:35.980
and run near real time
analysis on those data as well.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.360
In fact, you can run
a query on BigQuery,

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.757
which has got 100
billion rows of data,

00:10:42.757 --> 00:10:44.590
and get a result within
a couple of seconds.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:46.150
It's that fast.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:49.060
And finally, once you
have stored all this data,

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:52.480
and have run some
basic analysis,

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.970
you can then apply
Cloud Machine Learning

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.910
to get some more interesting
and more sophisticated

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.740
insights out of it as well.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:06.150
So the Industrial Revolution
is already upon us.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:11.010
And what we have built
so far is Cloud services,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.600
which can be applied
to build really

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.310
interesting applications.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:18.240
Now what I'm going to do is
I'm going invite our friends

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.260
and partners to showcase
to you some of the example

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:25.250
demos they have built
using our Cloud platform,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.440
which is planning to solve many
of the industrial application.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.020
First I want to
invite Willem, who

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.239
is CEO of Arden Technologies.

00:11:33.239 --> 00:11:35.280
And they've built really
interesting applications

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:36.360
for manufacturing.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.055
Willem?

00:11:38.055 --> 00:11:41.520
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:42.800
WILLEM SUNDBLAD: Thank you.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:43.580
My name is Willem.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:45.470
And as Indranil said,
I'm CEO and co-founder

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.070
of Odin Technologies.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:51.660
How many here had heard the
term Industry 4.0 before?

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:52.360
Oh.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.710
Quite a lot.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.330
Thanks for that introduction.

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:58.280
There was a great survey done
by German manufacturers--

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:00.370
1,000 of them--
to gauge the level

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:04.260
of know-how and excitement about
this new trend and buzzword.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:08.620
About 50% of them said that
Industry 4.0 was actively

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.860
being planned and
discussed in their plants.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.630
Around 80% said it
was going to have

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.240
a long and lasting
dramatic impact

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.110
on the industry as a whole.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.470
About the same 80% said
it was going to give them

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:22.120
a competitive edge.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:25.872
But only 27% said that they
felt like they knew what it was.

00:12:25.872 --> 00:12:27.580
So half that people
are talking about it.

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.704
Everyone think it's going
to be awesome, especially

00:12:29.704 --> 00:12:30.520
for themselves.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.880
But very few actually feel
like they know what it is.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.210
In our opinion, Industry 4.0 and
industrial internet of things--

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:43.150
all it is, it's about leveraging
data and technology to take

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.670
better decisions faster.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.890
Because the problems that
are facing manufacturers,

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.620
the industrial challenges,
or their business outcomes

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:54.370
and goals haven't changed.

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:59.380
Haven't changed in the
past 10, 20 or 100 years.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:02.950
They still want to make more
as efficiently as possible.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:04.510
Use as little
energy as possible,

00:13:04.510 --> 00:13:07.125
use as little material
or time as possible.

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.810
If you look at US
manufacturing as a whole,

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:16.217
it's easy to think of only the
top brand name manufacturers

00:13:16.217 --> 00:13:17.550
when you think of manufacturing.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.570
The Tesla's of the world, the
Rolls-Royce's of the world.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:23.160
But the US factories
had an output in 2015

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.425
of 6.2 trillion dollars.

00:13:25.425 --> 00:13:27.930
That's 36% of GDP.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.080
Most of those companies are
not Rolls-Royce or Tesla.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.480
Most of them are material
processing companies

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:36.890
making components for
these larger manufacturers.

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:40.230
A normal car is built up of
30,000 components all coming

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.980
from a complex
network of suppliers.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.580
And take the US
plastics industry

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:46.830
as an example, where
we've gotten our start,

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.860
and we have our biggest
focus right now.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.620
16,000 factories
in the US, where

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.800
most of the data that they
collect and analyze and use

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:59.600
for continuous improvement
is done with pen and paper,

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.900
clipboards and timers, to try
to understand and optimize

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.400
their production.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:08.827
So if they have a problem with
a rejection, a quality problem,

00:14:08.827 --> 00:14:10.410
or they're trying
to optimize material

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.993
consumption of the settings, or
trying to understand a machine

00:14:12.993 --> 00:14:13.740
failure--

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.530
When that problem happens,
they send engineers out there

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:20.310
to monitor it with pen and paper
to take the data in, and then

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:23.040
formulate a hypotheses, and try
to do the root cause analysis.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.270
So problem-solving can
take weeks to months.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:31.110
I'm not saying that there is
no technology in manufacturing,

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.880
but the technology
that's out there

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:37.830
has been only accessible to
those 1% of manufacturers.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.060
Hardware has always been
built on programmable logic

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:41.010
controllers.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:42.970
You know, stemming from the 70s.

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:47.340
Not a lot of people are
studying letter logic anymore.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.180
The design of
software usually looks

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.820
like it's from the 90s at best.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:55.670
Sometimes from 80s,
more resembling MS-DOS.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.350
Software is usually
hosted locally.

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:01.410
And it's always extremely
expensive and difficult

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:02.430
to implement.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.750
It can take months to years.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:08.390
But it doesn't have to
be that way anymore.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.400
Through ubiquity of networks,
drastic decreasing cost

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.350
of electronics, and
Cloud computing,

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:14.940
coupled with the
tremendous tools

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:18.180
and power that's available now,
superior industrial technology

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.130
or superior
technology, in general,

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.370
is now available to
manufacturers large and small

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:24.990
in all different verticals.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:28.100
The technology that has helped
us in our consumer lives

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.750
to click more links and
buy more stuff on Amazon

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.810
can now be used to eliminate
waste in manufacturing.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.580
That's our core mission.

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:38.730
We founded Odin to
empower manufacturers

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.910
with data and tools so that
they can eliminate their waste

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:42.930
in their production.

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.240
Decrease or eliminate
scrap rates.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:47.010
Decrease or eliminate
machine failures

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.540
and unplanned downtime.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.820
Optimize settings and
material consumption,

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:55.050
so they can make more with less.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:16:00.050
And we do this by delivering a
device built on Raspberry Pi.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.990
That we can communicate with
most industrial machinery

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:06.140
or external sensors,
capture data in real time

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:08.270
from the machines,
send wirelessly

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:10.700
to our Cloud-based
Analytics platform.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.590
And there our
customers or process

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.780
engineers, quality engineers,
industrial engineers,

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:18.050
manufacturing engineers, or
executives at these facilities

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:20.370
are using the platform daily
to solve quality problems

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:23.200
and machine failures.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.660
A process that normally
took weeks, or months,

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.430
now takes minutes.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.650
And we do this completely
on Google Cloud.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:33.630
So the millions and
millions of data

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:37.210
point that we take in per
production line, per day,

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.360
is ingested with Pub/Sub.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.817
Our real time stream
processing framework

00:16:41.817 --> 00:16:42.900
lives in Container Engine.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.960
We're in the process of moving
that to Data Flow right now.

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:48.310
As an example of the stuff
that we can do there,

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.760
we can take laser measurements
of the output coming

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.040
from the machine,
calculate in real time

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:56.750
the volume of that product
with the speed of production--

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.100
we know the flow
rate of material.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:00.960
We also know what
density that plastic has,

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.780
so we can know how
much pounds of material

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.440
they're using per feet,
per hour, per RPM,

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.849
per percent horsepower in
real time and historically.

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:12.839
Their set points
and their actuals

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:15.505
means that you know
how much waste there is

00:17:15.505 --> 00:17:16.630
and where it's coming from.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:21.834
Let's switch laptops.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:28.210
So [INAUDIBLE] a
real time stream

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:32.060
of everything that's happening
out on the production line.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:39.400
The purple one is the target
of where they should be.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:43.390
The green one is the output
coming from the machines.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:45.570
You have the yellows,
the melt pressure.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.290
So how the material
is actually behaving

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:50.140
as it's leaving the machine.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:52.540
This can be put as an
HMI, as a human machine

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:54.890
interface, down on the
production line for people.

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:58.260
You can add whatever metrics you
want, original or calculated.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:17:59.940
You get a high
level overview over

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:04.150
your throughput utilization,
across lines, across factories,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:07.800
for execs to understand
more how they're running.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:09.380
You have complete traceability.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:12.430
So you can search through your
production, based on product,

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:16.270
based on time, based on
events that have happened.

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:19.540
So if an engineer is trying to
solve a problem, what they do

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:22.060
is they search for
whatever product

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:24.910
they were running, whatever
time frame it happened,

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:26.695
or for the event itself.

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:33.370
They can then open it up.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.510
I was slow when I
was recording this.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.785
And they can open
it up here, and

00:18:37.785 --> 00:18:39.160
problem-solving
and manufacturing

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.220
is like peeling the
layers of an onion.

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:42.911
You don't really
know where to start.

00:18:42.911 --> 00:18:45.410
But here they start with the
most important process variable

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:47.250
for them, the output diameter.

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.220
This is a cable
extrusion example.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:52.960
They looked at the melt
pressure of the plastic.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.410
They look at the melt
temperature of the plastic.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:59.050
They look at the nominal to
see where they actually were.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:02.870
You can zoom down to a second
level granularity on the data.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:06.220
So here they've identified
a certain problem

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:08.970
with the fluctuation
of the cable.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.350
The diameter was
out of tolerance,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.750
which causes return loss
in the cable, which means

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:15.980
that the customer can't use it.

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:18.160
So you can then, A,
identify exactly where

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.220
that happened, so you can
cut it out and sell the rest.

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.060
You can also understand
why it happens.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.850
So in this case,
it looked like it

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:28.375
was the motor load that all of
a sudden changed the pressure.

00:19:28.375 --> 00:19:30.430
It changed the amount
of plastic that ended up

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:33.950
going out on that cable.

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:36.410
You can also, once you've
identified something,

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.600
you can select
certain parts of it,

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:42.420
either for statistical
processing or to label it.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:45.580
Labeling allows you to find it
again, share and collaborate,

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:48.510
but also to train up
machine learning models

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.970
so that both different
states or your production

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:54.390
can be classified automatically,
and different failures

00:19:54.390 --> 00:19:55.780
can be predicted in the future.

00:20:09.331 --> 00:20:10.080
I think that's it.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:13.500
I think we can switch over
to the presentation again.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:22.380
So in the same way that we build
tools and capabilities that

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:25.350
allows our customers to
run their factories more

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:28.140
efficiently and build
better products,

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.120
Google Cloud Platform
does that for us.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:34.530
We can move a lot faster
and build better products

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:38.370
for our customers by leveraging
the tools that Google Cloud

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.170
Platform provides.

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:42.090
We also know that we
can scale comfortably

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:46.410
throughout this year,
going for tens of thousands

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:49.860
of machines and
thousands of factories

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:54.420
without having to worry about
scaling up Bigtable or our data

00:20:54.420 --> 00:20:57.950
pipeline itself.

00:20:57.950 --> 00:20:59.437
And that's it for me.

00:20:59.437 --> 00:21:00.270
Thank you very much.

00:21:10.874 --> 00:21:12.540
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Willem.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:16.770
So you saw how Willem
and his company

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:20.640
are using Google
Cloud to really solve

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.730
a critical problem for
manufacturing, where they can

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.850
now have a view of exactly
how their machines that are

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:27.870
operating.

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.640
And they can take more
predictive action,

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:32.940
proactive action, rather
than waiting for days

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:34.620
and using clipboards
to figure out

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:36.910
where things are going wrong.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:41.210
The other area where we
see a lot of opportunity

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:45.330
and, in terms of where Cloud
and IoT can really help,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.950
is in asset-tracking.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.930
And we've been
working very closely

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.370
with our friends and
partners at Intel

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:54.570
to build some interesting
applications and demos for you

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:55.530
guys to share with.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:58.350
So let me to invite Giby
Raphael from Intel, who's going

00:21:58.350 --> 00:21:59.617
to talk about asset-tracking.

00:21:59.617 --> 00:22:00.116
Giby?

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:04.746
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:04.746 --> 00:22:06.530
GIBY RAPHAEL: Good afternoon.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:09.230
So I run the largest
segment at Intel.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.975
Let me begin the presentation
with three stories.

00:22:19.157 --> 00:22:19.990
Let's go with story.

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:23.660
So the first story comes from
our own backyard, from Intel.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:27.320
Intel is the world's number
fourth largest supply chain.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:30.200
We ship more than a
billion products a year

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:33.650
across hundreds of countries
and thousands of places.

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:38.420
About 30% to 40% of that
is fabrication equipment.

00:22:38.420 --> 00:22:39.800
And we have problems.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:46.130
We ship this box-sized
equipments, and this is 2016.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:50.690
We still use ink sensors, analog
ink sensors, to send stills.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:52.550
These boxes cannot be tilted.

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:55.850
These boxes have a wide
vibration threshold.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.850
And it loses calibration if
it exceeds the threshold.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:01.560
So we stick on a tilt sensor
with two compartments.

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:03.810
If the ink leaks from one
compartment, the other--

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.120
we know it tilted.

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:09.050
But we don't know how many times
it tilted, where it tilted,

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:12.980
how it tilted, so that we can
use that information next time.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.800
Even worse, since a
person is involved

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.770
in looking at and reporting
it, often times it doesn't get

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:19.700
reported.

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:22.070
We have had stories
where we ship these boxes

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:25.760
across international
borders, across countries,

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:29.030
and we have to bring it
back for calibration.

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:31.670
Obviously, Intel can
afford shipping costs

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:32.600
to bring the box back.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:35.990
But what we cannot afford
is if the fab gets shut down

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:37.700
for a day or two
because of this,

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.950
that's millions or tens of
millions of dollars in loss

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:41.480
for us.

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:44.200
The second story comes
from a flower retailer

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:44.960
in New Hampshire.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:48.160
So last year during
Valentine's Day,

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:51.760
he got a truckload of
flowers from Florida.

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:55.000
The flowers got spoiled
around Texas, midway.

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:57.490
Nobody knew about it.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:59.560
The night before
Valentine's Day,

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.140
he got a truckload
of rotten flowers.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:05.410
$50,000 written off.

00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:06.770
It's probably no big deal.

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:08.670
It's a big retailer.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:12.130
But what he couldn't afford
is the loss of reputation.

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:12.630
Right?

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:13.860
The single most
important day when

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:16.151
you're supposed to sell
flowers, he ran out of flowers.

00:24:16.151 --> 00:24:18.570
Those customers are
not coming back.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.390
The third story comes-- and hits
a little closer to the heart.

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:25.110
The 2014 Ebola epidemic
in West Africa--

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.990
a plane load of
vaccines got spoiled.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.070
Nobody noticed it.

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:32.280
And by the time they
realized it, it was too late.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:35.430
There was no time for plan
B or a contingency plan.

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:37.420
People died.

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:40.360
So these are not
isolated incidents.

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:45.320
But it happens every
day across the world.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:47.930
70% of all companies
in the world

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:50.330
experienced a
supply-chain disruption

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:52.700
over the last 12
months and 20% of them

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:55.900
went out of business
because of that.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:59.410
Last year, approximately
76 billion packages

00:24:59.410 --> 00:25:00.880
were shipped across the world.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:04.120
And 30%-- that's more
than 20 billion packages--

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:06.740
were damaged, delayed, or lost.

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:12.050
$60 billion of cargo value
was stolen last year.

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:14.090
On average-- this
is mind-blowing--

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:17.270
every single day, on
average, in the US two

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:19.280
cargo thefts happen on average.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:22.400
And the value of the theft
is in excess of $200,000.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:23.630
I grew up in India.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:26.540
65% of fruits and
vegetables in India

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:28.790
doesn't reach out
from the farm, right?

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:30.830
At that magnitude,
we're looking at making

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.500
a dent on world hunger.

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:37.790
We believe that using
real time technology,

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:41.870
real time tracking
of the location,

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:45.200
the condition and security
at the package level

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:47.600
can solve this,
can bring it down.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:49.820
But why hasn't it
been done before?

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:51.530
Why is nobody doing it?

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:52.790
Three reasons.

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:54.410
Cost, cost, cost.

00:25:54.410 --> 00:25:55.040
Right?

00:25:55.040 --> 00:26:00.020
Once I put a decent compute, the
memory, and a modem in a box,

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:02.960
it'll now cost me $100 to $400.

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:05.270
I cannot put them
on every package.

00:26:05.270 --> 00:26:07.800
It's too expensive.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.660
But, unfortunately,
all these problems

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:10.920
happen at the package level.

00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:12.990
Theft happens at
the package level.

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:15.240
We send armed guards
in certain countries

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.556
when we ship our
server processors.

00:26:17.556 --> 00:26:18.930
People run away
with the package.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:20.936
Each package is $100,000.

00:26:20.936 --> 00:26:22.560
Tampering happens at
the package level.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:27.010
Tilts, vibration has to be
sensed at the package level.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:28.469
That's just the Capex cost.

00:26:28.469 --> 00:26:29.760
And then there's the Opex cost.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.641
Shipping is one way,
point A to point B.

00:26:31.641 --> 00:26:33.640
But these things have to
be brought back, right?

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:36.010
So you need to close
the loop there.

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:38.620
And especially if it's
across international borders,

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:40.360
40% of the time it's
not coming back.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:43.786
Even when it comes back,
you have breakage, delay,

00:26:43.786 --> 00:26:45.160
you have to pay
for the shipping.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:47.590
It's a mess.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:51.560
That's when we looked
into IoT and Cloud.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:52.060
Right?

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:53.200
How can we resolve it?

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:57.610
So we invented a platform with
the vision of bringing down

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:00.520
the unit cost of tracking
to [? subtend ?] dollars

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:01.390
to begin with.

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:03.970
And, obviously, as
everything else it scales--

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:06.380
the price drops with scale.

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:09.360
Let me highlight three
features of this platform.

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:13.670
After that, Josh will come and
demonstrate our prototype here.

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:15.052
Number one, we looked at it--

00:27:15.052 --> 00:27:17.260
at the package level, what
do you need to sense this?

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:19.301
So we need a basic computer
at the package level.

00:27:19.301 --> 00:27:21.200
An MCU level compute.

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:23.024
And sensors.

00:27:23.024 --> 00:27:24.690
Then we looked at,
hey, in order to take

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:27.107
the data from the package to
the Cloud, what do you need?

00:27:27.107 --> 00:27:28.440
I cannot put a long-range radio.

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.689
I cannot put a modem in there.

00:27:29.689 --> 00:27:31.180
It's too expensive.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:33.310
Can we put a short-range
radio in there, right?

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:37.240
But, by definition, problem
is it's short range.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.000
So we invented the world's--

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.330
probably one of
those smallest power

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:43.920
mesh network out
there with multi-hop.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.610
That will now solve the range
problem to a large extent.

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.440
It also solves the
density problem,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.690
where I can now have 1,000
sensors talking to one modem

00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:55.830
to upload the data.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:59.280
And finally, a page
from cell phone analogy,

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:01.710
we have a software IP
wherein all the sensors can

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:05.790
move from one modem to the
other, which basically means

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.920
I can have fixed modems, fixed
gateways in trucks, in planes,

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:13.280
in trains, in
warehouses, in airports.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:15.260
And once I build
this infrastructure,

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:18.150
I'm only paying for the
sensor at the package level.

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:21.440
Josh will now demonstrate
a prototype we have here.

