But we are working
with customers--

00:38:32.867 --> 00:38:34.450
for example, on the
smart grid sensor.

00:38:34.450 --> 00:38:36.283
It's where we work with
microstate interface

00:38:36.283 --> 00:38:37.130
for sensors.

00:38:37.130 --> 00:38:40.160
They produce up to
512 values per cycle,

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:43.420
which could be like maybe
30 cycles per second,

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:45.030
ingesting of data all the time.

00:38:45.030 --> 00:38:47.450
So we want to discover
information from that data

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:49.980
while it's streaming in.

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:52.770
So conventional
utility systems are not

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:55.290
able to keep up with
this data diversity.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:57.570
So it's not only about
the sensory data itself,

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:00.580
but how can you add context
and arrange the data itself

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:01.830
that's coming from the sensor.

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:05.190
Or by using data, for example,
from the asset management

00:39:05.190 --> 00:39:09.150
system, data from other sensors
nearby, or maybe social data.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:09.940
Even weather data.

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:12.210
Sociological,
demographical information.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:13.740
Anything that's
available that can

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.550
enrich the context
of your device.

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:18.300
Or of your time sheets,
of the data sets,

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:21.030
would increase the value.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:24.270
So utilities are
facing a change.

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:26.820
Maybe I mentioned it before,
the energy transformation.

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:29.760
It means they have to change
from a commodity-driven

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.350
approach-- business model--

00:39:31.350 --> 00:39:33.000
to more data-driven
business model.

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:34.635
So it means they have to
research other possibilities

00:39:34.635 --> 00:39:35.700
of how to work with data.

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.950
Imagine, top of mind,
energy will be free.

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:41.430
So you don't pay for your
kilowatt hours anymore.

00:39:41.430 --> 00:39:44.190
How are they going to
build a business model?

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:45.060
So that's the key.

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:48.000
That's what they're
struggling with currently.

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:50.880
So that's why Energy
Works made it our mission

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:54.480
to enable this energy evolution
by uncovering and monetizing

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:56.670
this hidden value in the data.

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.864
And we have a process
in place to handle this.

00:39:59.864 --> 00:40:01.530
So it starts with the
injection of data.

00:40:01.530 --> 00:40:04.080
Because we want to control
the entire process,

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:07.380
from ingestion all the
way up to monetization.

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:10.080
Because data quality is
most of the time very poor,

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:13.050
so poor that you cannot work
with certain business cases

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:16.080
or use-cases, and the
product cannot become viable

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:17.220
because of that.

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:19.830
So injection of data from
sensors, from actuators,

00:40:19.830 --> 00:40:23.310
from smart meters either
push towards your platform,

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:26.700
or we read it in by
connecting to those devices,

00:40:26.700 --> 00:40:29.760
either via our RUT
gateways or hidden systems,

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:32.610
or directly connecting to those
devices like, for example,

00:40:32.610 --> 00:40:35.790
solar inverters or even
charging points for smart meters

00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:36.521
directly.

00:40:36.521 --> 00:40:37.770
And then we crunched the data.

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:38.902
We add value to the data.

00:40:38.902 --> 00:40:40.110
We try to correlate the data.

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:42.759
We try to discover
patterns in this data.

00:40:42.759 --> 00:40:44.050
So we can learn from this data.

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:46.320
So one of the things we're
going to show you today

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:49.080
is the interactive mode of our
platform, where we can actually

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:50.770
explore the data,
work with the data,

00:40:50.770 --> 00:40:52.650
see what's happening
by zooming in,

00:40:52.650 --> 00:40:55.540
interactively,
looking at the data.

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:57.790
While having said this, we
can also run the same thing

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:58.900
at Cloud Scale.

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:01.070
So once you have found
that perfect mole,

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:02.875
that perfect algorithm--

00:41:02.875 --> 00:41:04.750
maybe your data scientist
is working on this.

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:06.730
And then with one
click of a button,

00:41:06.730 --> 00:41:09.250
you can run this at Cloud Scale
across millions of devices,

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:11.034
while data is
being streaming in.

00:41:11.034 --> 00:41:12.700
And finally, of course,
you can monetize

00:41:12.700 --> 00:41:15.280
this perfect application
or algorithm that you

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:17.102
want to launch in this market.

00:41:20.050 --> 00:41:22.060
Before I go into
that, first I want

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:24.020
to explain to you the
energy value chain.

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:27.630
So, starting from generation
all the way up to supply,

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:31.660
Energy Works facilitates
this entire process

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:34.810
by enabling our
data intelligence

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:36.110
across the entire value chain.

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:38.260
So for example, on
the generation side,

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:41.900
we work with providers that
provide renewable energy.

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:43.644
So they have to
manage that energy.

00:41:43.644 --> 00:41:45.310
They want to see
what's being generated.

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:46.450
What's happening
with the generation?

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:46.949
Et cetera.

00:41:46.949 --> 00:41:48.310
They want to predict it.

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:52.100
On the transmission side
and the distribution side,

00:41:52.100 --> 00:41:53.476
grid and balances.

00:41:53.476 --> 00:41:54.850
Congestion
management, et cetera.

00:41:54.850 --> 00:41:56.680
There's a lot of data
coming there as well.

00:41:56.680 --> 00:41:59.380
Some of the data is not real
time, like metro-oriented

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:01.790
or maybe even a week old.

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:04.330
But some of the data
can be streamed in live.

00:42:04.330 --> 00:42:07.570
Then we're going to talk
about energy deal analytics

00:42:07.570 --> 00:42:09.670
use-case today, which
we're going to demonstrate.

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:10.930
So let me skip that part.

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:13.490
And then last but not
least, the supply side,

00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:15.040
which involves
consumer engagement,

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:17.690
any kind of applications,
and time of use rates.

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:27.080
Energy Works is built on top
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:42:27.080 --> 00:42:29.580
Energy Works believes in the
server-less architecture,

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:32.810
which means we don't want to
work with servers directly

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:33.500
ourselves.

00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:35.150
And of course, in
the end, our software

00:42:35.150 --> 00:42:38.032
runs on servers
somewhere in the Cloud,

00:42:38.032 --> 00:42:39.740
but we don't want to
be bothered with it.

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:41.739
So that's why we don't
use, for example, Compute

00:42:41.739 --> 00:42:43.250
Engine or Contain Engine.

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:46.010
Everything is running on
platform as a service.

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:48.110
So on the collect
side, we use App Engine

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:49.850
for our REST-based API.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:52.630
If customers want to integrate
directly with our platform.

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:54.380
And sometimes, you
also use Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:42:54.380 --> 00:42:55.860
especially for
utilities that want

00:42:55.860 --> 00:42:59.390
you to have a
streaming possibility

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:01.970
or streaming in data.

00:43:01.970 --> 00:43:05.810
So the second step,
the processing side--

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:08.240
we use Data Flow
very intensively.

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:09.800
We use it for
injection of data, we

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:13.160
use it for cleansing of the
data, manipulating the data,

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:16.790
checking or applying
validation rules, et cetera.

00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:19.495
We use Data PROC as well for
short and small analytics.

00:43:19.495 --> 00:43:20.870
Then on the storage
side, you see

00:43:20.870 --> 00:43:23.360
a lot of different clous
storage platforms that we use.

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.250
You might wonder, why is this?

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:28.520
So we believe that data,
with certain characteristics,

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:30.980
should be stored in
a data store that

00:43:30.980 --> 00:43:33.510
is best suitable for
those kind of data sets.

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:35.540
For example, relational data--

00:43:35.540 --> 00:43:38.060
you shouldn't store in Bigtable.

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:40.100
You could do it in
Data Store, maybe,

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:41.960
if you split it
up in key values.

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:45.680
But credibility of
data is very important.

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:48.540
So we store time series
data in Bigtable.

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:50.790
We store relational information
in Cloud SQL, which we

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:52.880
are upgrading to Cloud Spinner.

00:43:52.880 --> 00:43:55.340
We use Cloud Storage as an
intermediate staging store

00:43:55.340 --> 00:43:56.224
as well.

00:43:56.224 --> 00:43:58.140
And for example, if you
are streaming in data,

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:00.230
and you want to apply
logic on data itself,

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:01.730
it's very important
to realize which

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:03.372
data store you're going to use.

00:44:03.372 --> 00:44:04.830
Not to mention the
cost, of course,

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:06.380
if you use certain products.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:08.805
For example, with Data
Store, you pay per operation.

00:44:08.805 --> 00:44:11.680
With Bigtable, you pay
for the nodes you use

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:14.060
and the amount of
concurrent connections.

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:17.080
Then last, but not
least-- the analyze side--

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:19.520
we use App Engine again
to expose this information

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:20.720
to the outside world.

00:44:20.720 --> 00:44:23.750
So customers can authenticate
against our API, which we tidly

00:44:23.750 --> 00:44:26.120
integrate normally with
their existing platforms.

00:44:26.120 --> 00:44:29.210
We use Cloud ML for
analyzing the data itself.

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:31.670
We have some sophisticated
testing-flow models.

00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:33.590
For example, for
anomaly detection.

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:38.860
And we use BigQuery for
certain reporting capabilities.

00:44:38.860 --> 00:44:42.239
So my colleague, Erik, is going
to step on stage in a minute.

00:44:42.239 --> 00:44:44.030
He's going to explain
some of the use-cases

00:44:44.030 --> 00:44:44.710
we have been doing.

00:44:44.710 --> 00:44:46.418
And one of the use-cases
we've been doing

00:44:46.418 --> 00:44:49.220
is a risk management
case for a Fortune 500

00:44:49.220 --> 00:44:52.080
retailer in US based in Texas.

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:54.830
We automated their-- this is
critical-- process for pricing

00:44:54.830 --> 00:45:00.060
and forecasts so they could
get better insights in risk

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:01.680
and risk reduction.

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:08.750
So risks that they
sometimes run into

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:11.120
could be 100 million
or 200 million.

00:45:11.120 --> 00:45:13.985
So they service like, a
hundred thousands, or maybe

00:45:13.985 --> 00:45:15.740
a million customers.

00:45:15.740 --> 00:45:19.130
C and I, commercial industrial,
but also residential customers.

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:21.787
And they are buying
energy from the market.

00:45:21.787 --> 00:45:23.120
Short-term, mid-term, long-term.

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:25.190
Could be hundreds of
millions of dollars.

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:27.500
So we provided them
better insights

00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:30.070
with pluggable, validation,
estimation, and editing

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:30.740
capabilities.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.770
That's a specific energy
industry term by the way.

00:45:33.770 --> 00:45:36.140
But also with benchmarking
and scoring capabilities.

00:45:36.140 --> 00:45:38.330
So during the entire
process, we are

00:45:38.330 --> 00:45:41.270
able to see what the quality
is of the data itself,

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:44.100
with some scoring indexes
and scoring figures.

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:46.775
So we could provide an
accurate energy demand and load

00:45:46.775 --> 00:45:47.275
forecast.

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:53.250
We were able to reduce their
process from eight hours,

00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:56.700
or sometimes even longer,
to minutes or even seconds,

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:59.610
from six solutions to
one centralized view.

00:45:59.610 --> 00:46:02.010
So, for example, the load
analysts [? system ?]

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:04.740
that they're working with
there with 40 people doing

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:06.600
10 customers a month--
they could suddenly

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:09.070
do more than $1,000 a month.

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:11.970
So from inaccurate data to
measurable data quality,

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:14.775
with a bottom line impact
of $50 million plus.

