And we can enable particular
outlier detections

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:11.750
that we created in this,
our tensorflow model.

00:51:16.550 --> 00:51:26.210
And this is a live demo.

00:51:26.210 --> 00:51:26.775
A problem.

00:51:29.465 --> 00:51:30.340
I'm running it again.

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:38.460
So what it's doing now,
it's reading the data again.

00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:40.920
It's providing all
the validation rules,

00:51:40.920 --> 00:51:45.160
and it's estimating where
the KPI's are not met.

00:51:45.160 --> 00:51:47.494
And then, it will
visualize the results.

00:51:47.494 --> 00:51:48.285
And we see it here.

00:51:58.830 --> 00:51:59.545
OK.

00:51:59.545 --> 00:52:03.230
The tensorflow Flow
Model detected outliers

00:52:03.230 --> 00:52:05.160
in this data flow.

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:10.150
And I can drill down on
this particular analysis.

00:52:10.150 --> 00:52:12.860
I can zoom in on this data.

00:52:12.860 --> 00:52:15.850
And this is an outlier.

00:52:15.850 --> 00:52:20.950
This is caused probably by
an industrial engine that

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:23.830
broke down for several
days and didn't

00:52:23.830 --> 00:52:28.630
consume the data that it should
consume in normal operation.

00:52:28.630 --> 00:52:31.240
And to be able to
create a forecast,

00:52:31.240 --> 00:52:35.590
based on this smart metadata,
we want to get rid of all

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:39.730
the outliers because
that's going to influence

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:44.620
the forecast, and then we cannot
create the right commercial

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:48.490
offer for the utility customer.

00:52:48.490 --> 00:52:52.790
So what an analyst can do now,
it's detected this outlier.

00:52:52.790 --> 00:52:57.760
And we can now
estimate the value

00:52:57.760 --> 00:53:00.610
as if this engine
never broke down.

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:03.460
So we are going to use
a like-day estimation.

00:53:03.460 --> 00:53:08.770
So it's searching for similar
days for the consumption

00:53:08.770 --> 00:53:11.260
in this particular meter.

00:53:11.260 --> 00:53:15.310
And now, the orange line
is reflecting the situation

00:53:15.310 --> 00:53:17.320
like this engine
never broke down.

00:53:17.320 --> 00:53:22.960
So this dip is reduced,
and the gaps are filled.

00:53:22.960 --> 00:53:28.360
We have a linear estimation to
fill out missing data, to fill

00:53:28.360 --> 00:53:30.430
out the gaps in the data.

00:53:30.430 --> 00:53:34.620
And in the end, we can show a
merge of all the estimations

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:38.030
that we have done for
this particular data set.

00:53:38.030 --> 00:53:46.480
And if I zoom it out, and
only showed the merge line,

00:53:46.480 --> 00:53:48.850
then this will be
the end result,

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:53.650
the improved quality of the
raw data that was fed in.

00:53:53.650 --> 00:53:58.720
And this can be used to
generate a forecast that can be

00:53:58.720 --> 00:54:02.710
used for commercial products.

00:54:02.710 --> 00:54:06.240
The analysts can also,
on the right side,

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:10.110
view the KPI's that belongs to
this estimation and validation

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:10.610
rules.

00:54:10.610 --> 00:54:13.510
So it can tweak around with
different validation rules,

00:54:13.510 --> 00:54:16.660
try different estimation
methods to make sure

00:54:16.660 --> 00:54:20.590
that the KPI's are met
to be able to generate

00:54:20.590 --> 00:54:22.466
the right forecast.

00:54:22.466 --> 00:54:26.530
So basically, this is what
I wanted to show you guys.

